2026-09-27T21:01:30.123081Z [] {'source_reference_id': 'DFAT-1110', 'source_id': 'austender', 'title': 'PNG: Western Province Partnership Mid-Term Review', 'source_url': 'https://www.tenders.gov.au/Atm/Show/a64467be-bb82-4228-85e7-5009fe1f162c', 'issuing_agency': 'Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade - Australian Aid Program', 'category': 'Request for Tender', 'status': None, 'publish_date': '2026-09-02', 'closing_date': '2026-09-28', 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': 'AUD', 'value_notes': 'Up to $220,000 plus Australian GST as required, applies to the lifetime of the contract.', 'location': 'Papua New Guinea (South Fly District, Kiunga, Delta Fly)', 'description': "The Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (DFAT) requires a Mid-Term Review (MTR) of the Western Province Partnership (WPP) in Papua New Guinea. The review will assess the WPP's progress, achievements, challenges, and provide actionable recommendations for the remainder of WPP Phase II (2026-2030). It will cover relevance, effectiveness, efficiency, sustainability, and cross-cutting performance, including Gender Equality, Disability, and Social Inclusion (GEDSI), subnational governance, and climate resilience. The scope includes WPP components such as Water Security and WASH, Economic Initiatives, Community Grants, Gender Empowerment and Advocacy, South Fly Ranger Program, provincial systems strengthening/technical assistance, and the WPP shipping/barge service. The primary geographic focus is South Fly District, but also includes Kiunga and Delta Fly.\n\nTenderers are expected to propose an appropriate team structure, level of effort (the stated 46 LOE days are indicative), and fieldwork methodology. DFAT will provide reasonable administrative support, including stakeholder introductions and advice on operating in Western Province. DFAT, through its managing contractor, will cover costs for meeting venues, catering, and fieldwork transportation within Western Province. The MTR is not expected to include a standalone primary quantitative beneficiary survey; a proportionate mixed-methods approach using existing data and targeted qualitative collection is sufficient. DFAT will provide access to relevant program documentation, monitoring data, administrative data, baseline studies, and budget/expenditure information.\n\nThe estimated contract value is up to AUD $220,000 (plus GST) for the lifetime of the contract, which includes an optional extension. The initial contract term is 6 months, from October 2026 to April 2027, with an option to extend for an additional 3 months, from April 2027 to July 2027.\n\nThe Approach to Market (ATM) was published on 2 September 2026 and closes on 28 September 2026 at 10:00 am (ACT Local Time). The MTR is expected to be delivered between October 2026 and April 2027, with fieldwork tentatively scheduled for 10-15 days around November 2026. The response form does not require an Executive Summary. Tenderers may include tables, charts, diagrams, and infographics within specified page limits (Part 3: 2 A4 pages, Part 4: 3 A4 pages, Part 5: 1 A4 page). Compliance with the Commonwealth Supplier Code of Conduct and other specific policies (Fraud, Anti-corruption, Child Protection, Preventing Sexual Exploitation Abuse and Harassment) is mandatory. The Commonwealth Contract Terms are non-negotiable.", 'summary_headline': 'DFAT seeks supplier for the Mid-Term Review of the Western Province Partnership in Papua New Guinea.', 'contact_name': 'Tender Secretariat, Australian High Commission Port Moresby', 'contact_email': 'Port-Moresby.Devprogramquality@dfat.gov.au', 'contact_phone': None, 'lodgment_address': 'www.tenders.gov.au', 'documents': [{'file_name': 'DFAT-1110 - PNG WPP MTR - Addendum 2.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \np.1 of 4 \n  \nADDENDUM 2 to the Approach to Market (ATM) for DFAT-1110 PNG Western Province Partnership (WPP) Mid-\nTerm Review released on 2 September 2026.   \n \nResponses to follow-up questions submitted to the mailbox: \n \nQuestion \nAnswer \n1 \nCould DFAT please confirm whether the \nindicative budget of AUD $220,000 (plus \nGST) applies only to the initial contract \nterm ending 5 April 2027, or is inclusive \nof the optional three-month extension? \nPlease also confirm whether the \nextension period should read 6 April \n2027 to 6 July 2027. \n \nThe AUD $220,000 (plus GST) applies to the lifetime of \nthe contract. There is no cost associated with the \nextension option. \n \nDFAT confirms that the extension period should read 6 \nApril 2027 to 6 July 2027. \n2 \nCould DFAT please confirm whether the \nstated 46 days of level of effort \nrepresents the total combined input for \nthe entire evaluation team or is \nindicative only, with tenderers expected \nto propose an appropriate team \nstructure and total level of effort? \n \nThe 46 LOE days are indicative. Tenderers should propose \nan appropriate team structure and level of effort based \non their methodology and assessment of the \nrequirements outlined in the Terms of Reference (ToR). \nThe suitability of the proposed approach, team \ncomposition and value for money will form part of the \nassessment of tender. \n \n3 \nIn addition to facilitating stakeholder \nintroductions, could DFAT please \nconfirm whether it will provide or fund \nmeeting venues, local transport, \nfieldwork coordination, and other \nlogistical support? Alternatively, should \nall such costs be included in the \nManaging Contractor?s proposed \nbudget? \n \nDFAT will provide reasonable administrative support, \nincluding stakeholder introductions and advice on \noperating in Western Province. \n \nDFAT through its Western Province Partnership managing \ncontractor will support costs for meeting venues and \ncatering, fieldwork transportation in Western Province \nand coordination \n \n4 \nCould DFAT please confirm which \nevaluation team members are expected \nto travel and participate in fieldwork, \nand provide an indicative itinerary, \nincluding the anticipated locations and \nnumber of community or site visits \nacross Port Moresby, South Fly, Kiunga, \nand Delta Fly? \n \nThe ToR does not specify which team members are \nrequired to travel. Tenderers should propose an \nappropriate fieldwork team and methodology based on \nthe evaluation objectives and available resources. While \nconsultations are expected in Port Moresby and Western \nProvince, including South Fly, Kiunga and Delta Fly, the \nfinal itinerary and number of community/site visits will \nbe agreed during inception. Tenderers should propose \nand budget for a fieldwork approach that provides \nadequate coverage of WPP implementation areas and \nprogram components. \n \n5 \nCould DFAT please confirm whether the \nindicative 10?15-day fieldwork period \nincludes international, domestic, and \nremote-area travel time? Please also \nclarify whether these travel days are \nincluded within the stated 46 days of \nThe indicative 10?15-day fieldwork period is intended as \na guide only and does not prescribe how travel time \nshould be treated. The 46 days of level of effort are \nlikewise indicative rather than a fixed requirement. \nTenderers should propose a realistic level of effort, \nincluding any time required for international, domestic \n\np.2 of 4 \n \nlevel of effort or may be budgeted \nseparately. \n \nand remote-area travel, and clearly state their \nassumptions in the budget and methodology. The \nappropriateness of the proposed approach and \nresourcing will be considered as part of the tender \nassessment. \n \n6 \nThe ToR states that ?due to the remote \nand challenging operating environment \nof the Western Province Partnership, \nsupport and advice are available to \nfacilitate movement around Western \nProvince to undertake this review?. \nCould you please clarify the scope and \nnature of the support available? This \nwill inform how we budget for \nfieldwork. \n \nDFAT will provide reasonable administrative support, \nincluding stakeholder introductions and advice on \noperating in Western Province. \n \nDFAT through its Western Province Partnership managing \ncontractor  will support costs for meeting venues and \ncatering, fieldwork transportation in Western Province \nand coordination.  \n \n7 \nThe ToR also states that fieldwork and \nconsultations are to take place from 11 \nNovember 2026 with 10-15 days LOE \nallocated across the team for this. Given \nit is stated also that all 4 team members \nwill need to participate in field work in-\ncountry, this equates to just 3-4 days \nper team member which is likely not \nenough time given the methodology \nproposed. Could the LOE for the full \nteam for the field work component \nplease be clarified? \n \nThe 46 LOE days are indicative. Tenderers should propose \nan appropriate team structure and level of effort based \non their methodology and assessment of the \nrequirements outlined in the ToR. The suitability of the \nproposed approach, team composition and value for \nmoney will form part of the assessment of tender. \n \n8 \nThe ToR specifies analysis of existing \nquantitative and qualitative monitoring \ndata alongside KIIs, FGDs/beneficiary \nconsultations and site observations. \nCould DFAT clarify whether the MTR is \nexpected to include new primary \nquantitative data collection with \nbeneficiaries/participants, or whether a \ntheory-based mixed-methods \nassessment drawing on existing \nquantitative data and purposively \nsampled primary qualitative evidence \nwould meet the Requirement? \nNo, the ToR does not require a standalone primary \nquantitative beneficiary survey. DFAT anticipates a \nproportionate mixed-methods approach that draws on \nexisting quantitative and qualitative program monitoring \ndata, complemented by targeted qualitative data \ncollection through KIIs, FGDs, beneficiary consultations \nand site visits. Tenderers may propose limited primary \nquantitative data collection if they consider it necessary \nto address the evaluation questions, but a theory-based \nmixed-methods assessment using existing quantitative \ndata and purposively sampled qualitative evidence would \nmeet the intent of the ToR. \n \n9 \nIf primary quantitative beneficiary data \ncollection is expected, could DFAT \nclarify the level(s) at which estimates \nare expected to be sufficiently robust - \nfor example, at overall WPP programme \nlevel, individual component level, \ngeographic level (e.g. South Fly, Kiunga, \nDelta Fly or Treaty/non-Treaty \ncommunities), and/or for specific \npopulation groups? \nDFAT does not currently expect the MTR to generate \nstatistically representative beneficiary estimates through \nprimary quantitative data collection. The intent is a \nproportionate mixed-methods evaluation that primarily \nutilises existing monitoring data, complemented by \ntargeted fieldwork. If tenderers propose primary \nquantitative data collection, they should justify the level \nof precision and representativeness required. Robust \nevidence at the overall WPP level is likely to be sufficient, \nwith component-, geography- or population-specific \nanalysis undertaken where feasible and supported by \n\np.3 of 4 \n \navailable data and resources. The MTR is not expected to \nproduce statistically representative estimates across all \ncomponents, locations or beneficiary groups. \n \n10 \nCould DFAT provide a component-wise \nbeneficiary/participant profile for WPP, \nincluding the number and type of direct \nand indirect beneficiaries by component \nand geography, and clarify the extent to \nwhich individuals, households, groups \nand communities participate in or \nbenefit from multiple WPP \ncomponents? Where populations \noverlap, is information available that \nenables participants or households to \nbe linked or de-duplicated across \ncomponents? \nDFAT is not in a position to provide a comprehensive \ncomponent-wise beneficiary and participant profile at \nthis stage of the procurement process.  \n \nA consolidated, de-duplicated beneficiary database is not \ncurrently available across all WPP components. Available \nmonitoring and administrative data will be provided to \nsupport analysis of beneficiary reach, participation and \ngeographic coverage. While component-level beneficiary \ninformation is available for many activities, the level of \ndetail and ability to identify overlap between participants \nacross multiple components varies by workstream and \nimplementing partner. The Evaluation Team should \nassess available datasets during inception and propose an \nappropriate approach to analysing beneficiary overlap \nand program reach. \n \n11 \nWill the Evaluation Team be provided \nwith component-level \nparticipant/beneficiary lists or another \nimplementation footprint containing \nsufficient information to construct a \nsampling frame, including geography, \ncomponent exposure, timing of \nparticipation and, where available, \nrelevant demographic characteristics? \nYes.  The Evaluation Team will be provided with available \nprogram monitoring and administrative data, including \nbeneficiary and participant information were held by \nimplementing partners. This is expected to include \nsufficient information to identify program locations, \nrelevant program components, and participant cohorts. \nHowever, the completeness and level of detail of \navailable data may vary by component and implementing \npartner. \n \n12 \nCould DFAT clarify what participant-\nlevel or disaggregated monitoring \ninformation will be available, including, \nwhere applicable, sex, age/youth status, \ndisability, location, \ncomponent/workstream exposure and \ntiming of participation? \nRelevant monitoring and administrative datasets \navailable to WPP will be provided to the Evaluation Team \nwhere appropriate. These may include participant-level \ninformation on sex, age, disability status, location, \ncomponent participation and timing of engagement, \nsubject to data availability and quality. As data collection \napproaches differ across components and partners, the \nlevel of disaggregation available may vary. The Evaluation \nTeam should assess available datasets during inception \nand propose an appropriate sampling and analysis \napproach accordingly. \n \n13 \nWere any landscape studies or baseline \nstudies conducted for WPP or its \ncomponents? If yes, will the Evaluation \nTeam have access to the reports and \nunderlying data? \nYes. The Evaluation Team will be provided with access to \nrelevant baseline studies, analytical reviews, evaluations \nand research that have informed WPP design and \nimplementation. This is expected to include documents \nreferenced in the ToR, such as the South Fly Resilience \nPlan Review, Western Province analytical work and \ncomponent-specific studies. Underlying data will be \nshared where available and appropriate, subject to data \nownership, privacy and confidentiality considerations \n\np.4 of 4 \n \nAll other information as set out in the DFAT-1110 ATM dated 2 September 2026 remains unchanged. \n--- End of Addendum 2 --- \n14 \nTo support efficient parameter KEQs 3 \nand 4, will the Evaluation Team have \naccess to both planned budgets and \nactual expenditure data, disaggregated \nwhere feasible by \ncomponent/workstream and major cost \ncategory, including grants, technical \nassistance, operating costs, and \nshipping/logistics? \nYes. The Evaluation Team will have access to relevant \nbudget, workplan and expenditure information to \nsupport assessment of efficiency and sustainability. \nWhere available, data will be provided by \ncomponent/workstream and major cost category, \nincluding grants, technical assistance, operating costs and \nshipping/logistics. The level of disaggregation may vary \ndepending on financial reporting systems, contractual \narrangements and data availability. \n \n15 \nThe CCS ATM Response Form indicates \nthat Part 2 ? Executive Summary is ?Not \nRequired.? Could DFAT please confirm \nthat an Executive Summary is therefore \nnot required as part of the response, \nand that the response should proceed \ndirectly from Part 1 to Part 3? \nDFAT confirms that an Executive Summary is not \nrequired. Please leave this section blank and proceed \ndirectly from Part 1 to Part 3.   \n16 \nThe ATM specifies page limits for Parts \n3, 4 and 5 of the response. Could DFAT \nplease confirm whether tables, charts, \ndiagrams and infographics may be used \nwithin these sections to present \ninformation concisely, provided that \nthey remain within the applicable page \nlimits and do not alter the structure or \nformatting of the CCS ATM Response \nForm? \nYes, you may include tables, charts, diagrams, \ninfographics, or any other visuals you consider necessary, \nprovided they are included within the page limit and do \nnot alter the structure or formatting of the CCS ATM \nResponse Form. \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1110 - PNG WPP MTR -- Addendum 1.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \np.1 of 1 \n  \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nADDENDUM 1 to the Request for Tender (RFT) for DFAT-1110 PNG: Western Province Partnership Mid-Term \nReview released on 2 September 2026.  \n \nThe procurement title has been updated to PNG: Western Province Partnership Mid-Term Review from Western \nProvince Partnership Mid-Term Review. \n \nResponses to follow-up questions submitted to the mailbox: \nAll other information as set out in the RFT dated 2 September 2026 remains unchanged. \n--- End of Addendum 1 --- \n \nQuestion \nAnswer \n1 \nWhat budget has been allocated for this \nMTR? \nUp to $220,000 plus Australian GST as required \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1110 - WPP MTR - Attachment A - TOR.pdf.txt', 'extracted_text': 'Terms of Reference: Western Province Partnership (WPP) Mid-Term Review 2026\n1. Background and orientation\nThe Western Province Partnership (WPP; 2023-2030) is a AUD114 million place-based partnership under the Australia?Papua New Guinea Subnational Program facility (APSP). WPP commenced in January 2023, transitioning from the South Fly Resilience Plan (2020 -2022) into a longer-term partnership aligned with the PNG?Australia Western Province Strategy (2022?2030). WPP was designed to support Australia?s standing as a partner of choice in Western Province by contributing to a more stable, healthy and prosperous province, through improved food and water security, community and provincial governance, inclusion and livelihoods. Key program approaches include technical advisers, training, facilitation, small-scale infrastructure and equipment.\nThe majority of WPP investment has focused on South Fly District, Western Province in recognition of the unique border relationship between Australia and Papua New Guinea along the Torres Strait, and the presence of other development partners in other parts of Western Province such as Ok Tedi Development Foundation and PNG Sustainable Development Program.  The APSP facility is the implementing mechanism for WPP. The APSP facility supports place-based programming, policy dialogue, and analysis in areas of mutual interest to Australia and Papua New Guinea. APSP and WPP entered Phase II in March 2026, with Phase II running to February 2030.\nThe WPP aligns and contributes to the Papua New Guinea-Australia Comprehensive Strategic and Economic Partnership and the Australia?Papua New Guinea Development Partnership Plan (2024?2029). It also remains a key mechanism for delivering the PNG?Australia Western Province Strategy (2022?2030), which applies a place-based approach and helps coordinate Australia?s development assistance in coordination with the Western Provincial Administration.\nThe WPP has four interrelated End-of-Program Outcomes (EOPOs):\nEOPO 1: Women and men participating in program investments have diversified livelihoods from agriculture and fisheries.\nEOPO 2: Improved community governance strengthens welfare, production, and resilience in targeted communities.\nEOPO 3: Women contribute to decision-making and take on leadership roles in targeted provincial, district, and community structures.\nEOPO 4: Policy and practice at provincial and district levels supports delivery of minimum priority services.\nWPP components, intermediate outcomes and outputs are outlined in the program logic (Annex A). The WPP also contributes towards four APSP Key Policy Priorities (KPP), each with their own Intermediate Outcomes (IO) under the APSP Performance Assessment Framework (PAF): \nKPP IO Subnational Governance:\xa0Targeted subnational [and regional] governments improve processes and institutional cooperation and coordination to enable improvements in service delivery and economic development.\nKPP IO Gender:\xa0Increased participation of women in decision-making, leadership, and the economy in APSP locations.\nKPP IO Disability:\xa0Increased participation of Persons with Disabilities (PWD) in subnational investment in APSP locations.\nKPP IO Climate Change:\xa0Programming in targeted provinces [and regions] protects conservation, livelihood, and economic assets from the impacts of climate change.\nA review of the South Fly Resilience Plan (2020 ? June 2022) , WPP?s predecessor program, was completed in 2022.\n2. Purpose and intended users\nThis Mid-Term Review (MTR) will assess WPP progress toward its intended outcomes, identify key achievements and challenges, and provide actionable recommendations to inform management decisions and program improvement and consolidation opportunities for the remainder of the WPP Phase II (2026 - 2030). The MTR will assess the relevance, effectiveness, efficiency, sustainability and cross-cutting performance (including GEDSI, subnational governance and climate resilience) of WPP?s approach and delivery.\nTable  Intended Users of the WPP Mid-Term Evaluation\n3. Scope of the evaluation\nThe evaluation will cover WPP implementation from program commencement (to the date of fieldwork (January 2023 ? July 2026). It will examine progress across WPP?s outcomes, WPP governance, delivery arrangements and six components: \nWater Security and Water, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH)\nEconomic Initiatives Program\nCommunity Grants Scheme\nGender Empowerment and Advocacy Program\nSouth Fly Ranger Program\nProvincial systems strengthening/technical assistance to provincial and district administrations\nThe WPP shipping / barge service is also in scope, given the significant and necessary use of shipping and logistics services as an enabling feature of the WPP. \nThe geographic focus will be South Fly District (including PNG Treaty and non-Treaty communities in our 40 target wards), however will cover all locations where WPP is implemented, including in Kiunga and Delta Fly. The evaluation should consider WPP?s interaction with other Australian and non-Australian actors in Western Province, to understand WPP performance, coherence and opportunities for sustainability.\n4. Key evaluation questions\nThe evaluation will address the following Key Evaluation Questions (KEQs), which may be refined in the Evaluation Plan in consultation with DFAT.\nTable  Proposed Key Evaluation Questions\n5. Methodology\nThe review team will propose a detailed methodology in an Evaluation Plan. DFAT expects a clear and structured mixed-methods approach that is proportionate to the evaluation questions and available resources, and that triangulates evidence across multiple sources. Methods are expected to include: \ninception meeting(s) with DFAT; \ndesktop review of key program documents (e.g. design documentation, MERLA and GEDSI strategies, six-monthly and annual reports, governance records, relevant research and partner data); \nkey informant interviews with DFAT, Abt Global managing contractor, implementing partners, provincial/district authorities, other GoPNG stakeholders, service providers, beneficiaries and other development actors; \nfocus group discussions and beneficiary consultations in selected communities (with separate consultations as appropriate for women, men, youth and people with disabilities); \nsite visits/observation of selected investments (where feasible);\nanalysis of quantitative and qualitative monitoring data to assess progress toward outcomes, inclusion and efficiency.\nThe MTR must be conducted in line with DFAT?s Development Evaluation Policy and the DFAT Design, Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning Standards, including ethical research and safeguarding expectations. \nThe Evaluation Plan should include a clear approach to informed consent, confidentiality, data security, and culturally appropriate engagement, including strategies to safely engage women, young people and other socially disadvantaged groups.\n6. Team composition and evaluation governance\nThe review team will be led by an independent Team Leader and include a mix of international evaluation and Papua New Guinean expertise. The team should collectively demonstrate experience evaluating complex development programs, ideally in PNG or comparable Pacific contexts, and expertise relevant to WPP?s thematic areas (community-driven development and governance, livelihoods/markets including agriculture, and evaluation methodology).\nThe review team should consider and prioritise the inclusion of PNG consultants to elevate local voices and perspectives. The team leader will have the opportunity to provide professional advice on the scope, questions and resources applied during contract negotiation. \nDFAT staff may also provide administrative support, including providing letters of introduction and supporting stakeholder introductions during the in-country visit. Due to the remote and challenging operating environment of the Western Province Partnership, support and advice are available to facilitate movement around Western Province to undertake this review.\nWe have summarised the required experience, skills, qualifications, and responsibilities for the proposed Evaluation Team in Table 3 below\nTable  Proposed Evaluation Team Composition\nMTR governance: The MTR will be managed by DFAT (Australian High Commission Port Moresby) through a nominated Evaluation Manager/Point of Contact. DFAT will provide oversight, facilitate access to documentation and stakeholders, and coordinate feedback on key deliverables. The evaluation team will provide regular updates to DFAT and, as appropriate, share emerging findings with key stakeholders (including Western Province counterparts and the Managing Contractor) to support validation and use of results, without compromising the independence of the evaluators? judgments.\n7. Evaluation schedule\nThe schedule below (tentatively October 2026? February 2027) is indicative and will be confirmed during contracting and finalisation of the evaluation plan. You should include a timeline of activities as part of the tender process which will be mutually agreed during the inception phase. Please find below indicative activities, reporting milestones and associated Level of Effort (LOE) in Table 4 below.\nTable .  Evaluation Activities, Key Deliverables, Milestones and Expected Level of Effort (LOE)\n8. Reporting and deliverables\nThe evaluation team will be responsible for the following deliverables:\nInception meeting: Confirm and finalise the purpose, scope, KEQs, stakeholders, methods, deliverables and timelines.\nDraft Evaluation Plan: Detailed methodology, sampling frame, stakeholder engagement plan, tools, analysis approach, quality assurance, ethics and safeguarding approach, and workplan. Not more than 7 pages excluding annexes. \nFinal Evaluation Plan: Incorporating DFAT feedback.\nPreliminary findings briefing / Aide Memoire: Summary of emerging findings and indicative recommendations, presented to DFAT and key stakeholders verbally through an Aide Memorie presentation and a written version. Not more than 10 pages, excluding annexes.\nDraft Evaluation Report: Findings, conclusions and actionable recommendations structured against the KEQs, including an executive summary. Not more than 30 pages, excluding annexes.\nFinal Evaluation Report: Incorporating consolidated comments and including a clear line of sight between evidence, findings, conclusions and recommendations. Not more than 30 pages, excluding annexes. \nPresentation of findings: A briefing to DFAT and key stakeholders (format to be agreed).\n9. List of relevant documents\nDFAT and the Managing Contractor will make available relevant documentation to the evaluation team. Indicative reference documents include:\nDFAT Design and monitoring, evaluation and learning standards\nDFAT Development Evaluation Policy 2023\nAustralia?s International Development Policy 2023 (include performing and delivery framework)\nAustralia ? Papua New Guinea Development Partnership Plan 2024 ? 2029\nPapua New Guinea?s Medium Term Development Plan IV 2023-2027\nWPP Investment Design Document and Theory of Change.\nWPP MERLA Plan and Monitoring & Evaluation Framework (and any updates).\nWPP GEDSI strategy and safeguarding-related guidance.\nWPP Sub-designs, concept notes, briefing notes and proposals. \nWPP six-monthly and annual progress reports, including relevant data sets and case studies 2022 - 2026. \nAPSP MERLA Plan and Performance Assessment Framework/related documents. \nGovernance records (Steering Committee minutes/decisions) and workplans and budgets.\nRelevant DFAT policies and guidance, including the DFAT Design, Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning Standards.\nAny relevant baseline studies, reviews and research underpinning WPP components (e.g., South Fly Resilience Plan Review, Western Province Analytical Review, Transport Value Chain Analysis, WASH, livelihoods, governance, gender).\nAnnex A: WPP Program Logic\t\n'}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1110 - WPP MTR -- CCS ATM.txt', 'extracted_text': 'Approach to Market\nTo establish Contract for Western Province Partnership Mid-Term Review 2026 \nReference ID: DFAT-1110 \nThis Approach to Market (ATM) is for the provision of: Western Province Partnership Mid-Term Review 2026. \nThe Commonwealth of Australia as represented by Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (the Customer) is seeking submissions for the provision of the services (the Requirement) as described in this ATM comprising:\nthis CCS ATM, including the Statement of Requirement\nthe CCS ATM Response Form\nthe CCS ATM Annexes (if any)\nthe Commonwealth ATM Terms\nthe Additional Contract Terms (if any)\nthe Commonwealth Contract Terms\nthe Commonwealth Contracting Suite Glossary and Interpretation.\nIn submitting a response, Potential Suppliers are required to comply with all requirements set out in the Commonwealth Approach to Market Terms (a copy of which is included in this document), and if successful, agree to enter into a contract which incorporates the Commonwealth Contract Terms available at https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-contracting-suite-ccs#ccs-terms.\nThe Selling to Government website provides information to help businesses understand how to sell to the Australian Government. If you have any general questions about how the Australian Government procures goods and services, please contact the Selling to Government team at\u202fsellingtogov@finance.gov.au.\xa0\nFor questions relating specifically to this ATM, please contact the ATM Contact Officer set out at A.A.5(a).\xa0\nStatement of Requirement\nA.A.1\tKey Information and Dates\nApproach to Market\nProposed Contract\nA.A.2\tThe Requirement\nSee attached terms of reference.\nA.A.2(a)\tCommonwealth Supplier Code of Conduct\nThe Commonwealth expects its suppliers to conduct themselves with high standards of ethics such that they consistently act with integrity and accountability.\xa0\xa0\nIf awarded a Contract, the Supplier must agree to comply with the Commonwealth Code of Conduct (Code) in accordance with the Commonwealth Contract Terms [Clause C.C.23].\xa0\nClause C.C.23 requires the Supplier to comply with the Code when performing its obligations under the Contract, and to ensure its personnel and Subcontractors comply with the Code. The clause also requires the Supplier to proactively monitor and assess compliance with the Code, and to notify the Customer immediately of any breach of the Code.\xa0\xa0\nIn accordance with the Commonwealth ATM Terms [Clause A.B.10], where requested by the Customer, Potential Suppliers should provide information demonstrating that they have appropriate policies, frameworks, or similar, in place to comply with the Code.\xa0\xa0\nFurther information on the Code can be found at: https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-supplier-code-conduct \nA.A.2(b)\tStandards\nPotential Suppliers must ensure that any goods and services proposed are capable of complying with all applicable Australian standards and any Australian and international standards specified in this Statement of Requirement. Potential Suppliers should note that they may be required to enable the Customer, or anindependent assessor, to conduct periodic audits to confirm compliance with those standards.\nWeb Content Accessibility\nThe Supplier must ensure that any website, associated material and/or online publications (where applicable) complies with the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines available at: https://www.w3.org/WAI/intro/wcag.\nPublications \nPublications and reports (if any) must be drafted to comply with the current version of the Australian Government Style Manual available at: https://www.stylemanual.gov.au/. \nA.A.2(c)\tSecurity Requirements \nN/A\nA.A.2(d)\tWork Health and Safety\nPrior to commencement of the Contract, the Customer?s Contract Manager and the Supplier?s Contract Manager will identify any potential work health and safety (WHS) issues anticipated to arise during the term of the contract and assign management of each issue identified to the party best able to manage it. For all issues assigned to the Supplier, the\xa0Supplier will provide the Customer with a WHS plan for approval and no work will commence until the plan is approved unless agreed in writing by the Customer.\nThroughout the Contract Term, the Customer and the Supplier will proactively identify and cooperate to manage any WHS issues that arise.\nA.A.2(e)\tDelivery and Acceptance\nThe Customer must accept or reject any deliverables under the Contract in accordance with the Commonwealth Contract Terms [Clause C.C.11].\nReports \nDuring the term of the Contract the Supplier must provide the Customer with reports as set out in the table below:\nA.A.2(f)\tMeetings\nThe Supplier will be required to attend meetings as follows:\nA.A.2(g)\tFacilities and Assistance Offered by the Customer\nThe Mid Term Review will be managed by DFAT (Australian High Commission Port Moresby) through a nominated Point of Contact and will facilitate introductions as required.\nA.A.2(h)\tCustomer Material\nSee attached terms of reference (in the TOR include reports etc that we will provide the supplier)\nA.A.3\tATM Distribution\nAusTender Distribution\nThis ATM and any updates are subject to the AusTender\xa0Terms\xa0of\xa0Use. Any updates to this ATM will be distributed via AusTender. \nAusTender is the Australian Government?s procurement information system. Access to and use of AusTender is subject to the AusTender Terms of Use. In participating in this ATM process, Potential Suppliers must comply with the AusTender Terms of Use and any applicable instructions, processes, procedures and recommendations as advised on AusTender.\nA.A.4\tLodgement of Responses\nAusTender\nResponses must be lodged electronically via AusTender before the Closing Time and in accordance with the Response lodgement procedures set out in this ATM documentation and on AusTender.\nAll queries for technical or operational support must be directed to the AusTender Help Desk. Contact details are available on the Contact Us page of the AusTender website.\nThe Closing Time will be displayed on the relevant AusTender webpage together with a countdown clock that displays, in real time, the time left until Closing Time (for more information refer to AusTender\xa0Terms\xa0of\xa0Use).\nFor the purpose of determining whether a Response has been lodged before the Closing Time, the\xa0countdown clock will be conclusive.\nResponse File Format, Naming Convention and Size\nThe Customer will accept Responses lodged in the following formats:\nMicrosoft Word (.docx)\nPDF (.pdf) \nRich Text Format (.rtf)\nThe Response file name/s should:\nincorporate the Potential Supplier?s full legal organisation name; and \nreflect the various parts of the bid they represent (where the Response comprises multiple files).\nResponses must be completely self-contained. No embedded files can be included. No hyperlinked or other material may be incorporated by reference.\nA.A.5\tCustomer?s Contact Officers\nA.A.5(a)\tATM Contact Officer\nFor all matters relating to this ATM, the Contact Officer is:\nName/Position:\tTender Secretariat, Australian High Commission Port Moresby\nEmail Address:\tPort-Moresby.Devprogramquality@dfat.gov.au \nNote: Question Closing Date and Time is set out at item A.A.1 [Key Information and Dates].\nA.A.5(b)\tComplaints Handling\nComplaints relating to this ATM should be directed to:\nInformation relating to the handling of complaints is available on the Customer?s website at: \nIf your issue is not resolved refer https://www.finance.gov.au/business/procurement/complaints-handling-charter-complaints for more information relating to complaints.\nAdditional Contract Terms\nAn executed contract will incorporate the Commonwealth Contract Terms and also the following Additional Contract Terms:\nA.C.1\tIntellectual Property\nThe Customer owns the Intellectual Property Rights in the Material created under the Contract. \nTo the extent the Supplier or a third party holds any Intellectual Property Rights in any existing Material, the Supplier hereby agrees to licence the Customer to enable the Customer to exercise full rights and interests in the Intellectual Property Rights in any Material provided under the Contract. The Supplier agrees to create, execute or sign any documents and perform all acts which may be necessary to allow the use of those rights by the Customer for any purpose.\nThe Customer grants to the Supplier a non-exclusive, non-transferable, irrevocable, royalty-free licence for the Contract Term to exercise the Intellectual Property Rights in the Material for the sole purpose of fulfilling its obligations under the Contract. The licence in this clause is subject to any conditions or limitations of third parties that the Customer notifies to the Supplier.\nIntellectual Property Rights in Goods provided under the Contract or pre-existing Intellectual Property of the Supplier, set out below (if any), will not change as a result of the Contract.\nA.C.2\tPayment Terms\nThe Customer must pay the amount of a Correctly Rendered Invoice to the Supplier within thirty\xa0(30)\xa0calendar days after receiving it, or if this day is not a Business Day, on the next Business Day.\nA.C.3\tFraud\nA.C.3.1\tThis clause replaces clause C.C.22.F [Fraud] of the Commonwealth Contract Terms. For the purposes of this clause, ?Fraud? means, in delivering the Goods and/or Services under the Contract, dishonestly obtaining a benefit, or causing a loss, by deception or other means, and includes suspected, alleged or attempted fraud.\nA.C.3.2\tThe Supplier must take all reasonable steps to prevent and detect Fraud in relation to the performance of this Contract. \nA.C.3.3\tSubject to A.C.3.4, if the Supplier becomes aware of any Fraud, it must report the matter to the Customer in writing within five (5) business days.\nA.C.3.4\tIf the Supplier is under a legal obligation not to report a Fraud to the Customer, but local police or other law enforcement agencies provide an exception to permit reporting, the Supplier must report a Fraud to the Customer within five (5) business days of the exception being granted.\nA.C.3.5\tThe Supplier must investigate all Fraud at the Supplier?s expense and in accordance with any reasonable directions or standards required by the Customer. After the investigation is finished, the Supplier must promptly report full details of any Fraud to:\nthe Customer (unless the Supplier is under a legal obligation not to report a Fraud to the Customer); and\nthe local police and any other appropriate law enforcement agency in the country where the incident occurred, unless the Customer agrees otherwise in writing. \nA.C.3.6\tIf the investigation finds Fraud by the Supplier or its officers, employees or agents, or the Supplier has failed to take reasonable steps to prevent Fraud by a subcontractor, the Supplier must, if directed by the Customer, promptly reimburse or compensate the Customer in full.\nA.C.3.7\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nA.C.4\tProhibited dealings\nA.C.4.1\tThe Supplier must ensure that it and its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors involved in delivering Goods and or Services under this Contract are not:\ndirectly or indirectly engaged in preparing, planning, assisting or fostering a terrorist act;\nlisted terrorist organisations for the purposes of the\xa0Criminal Code Act 1995\xa0(Cth) (details of listed terrorist organisations are available at:\nhttps://www.nationalsecurity.gov.au/Listedterroristorganisations/Pages/default.aspx);\nsubject to sanctions or similar measures under the\xa0Charter of the United Nations Act 1945\xa0(Cth) or the\xa0Autonomous Sanctions Act 2011\xa0(Cth) (details of individuals and entities are available at:\nhttps://dfat.gov.au/international-relations/security/sanctions/Pages/consolidated-list.aspx;\nlisted on the ?World Bank?s Listing of Ineligible Firms and Individuals? posted at:\xa0http://www.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement/debarred-firms;\nowned, controlled by, acting on behalf of, or at the direction of persons, or entities referred to in clauses A.C.4.1(a) to A.C.4.1(d) above; or\nproviding direct or indirect support, resources or assets (including any grant monies) to persons or entities referred to in clauses A.C.4.1(a) to A.C.4.1(e) above.\nA.C.4.2\tWhere the Supplier becomes aware that there are reasonable grounds to suspect it or any of its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors has or may have contravened any part of clause A.C.4, the Supplier must:\nnotify the Customer and confirm that information in writing as soon as possible, which must be no later than within 24 hours;\nimmediately take all reasonable action to mitigate the risks; and\ntake any other action reasonably required by the Customer.\nA.C.4.3\tThe Supplier must ensure that any subcontract entered into by the Supplier for the purposes of fulfilling its obligations under this Contract imposes on the Subcontractor the same obligations that the Supplier has under this clause\xa0A.C.4.\nA.C.4.4\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nA.C.5\tAnti-corruption\nA.C.5.1\tThe Supplier warrants that the Supplier, its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors have not made or caused to be made, or received or sought to receive, any offer, gift or payment, consideration or benefit of any kind, which would or could be construed as an illegal or corrupt practice, either directly or indirectly to any party, as an inducement or reward in relation to the execution or performance of the Contract.\nA.C.5.2\tThe Supplier must not, and must ensure that its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors, do not:\nmake or cause to be made, or receive or seek to receive, any offer, gift or payment, consideration or benefit of any kind, which would or could be construed as an illegal or corrupt practice, either directly or indirectly to any party, as an inducement or reward in relation to the performance of the Contract; and/or\nengage in any practice that could constitute the Australian offence of bribing a foreign public official\xa0in relation to the performance of the Contract.\nA.C.5.3\tIf the Supplier becomes aware of any practice, as described in A.C.5.2 (a) or (b), then it must report the matter to the Customer within five (5) business days.\nA.C.5.4\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nA.C.6\tChild Protection\nA.C.6.1\tThe Supplier must comply, and must ensure that its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors comply with the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade?s Child Protection Policy, accessible at http://www.dfat.gov.au/childprotection/.\nA.C.6.2\tThe Customer may conduct a review of the Supplier?s compliance with the Child Protection Policy. The Customer will give reasonable notice to the Supplier and the Supplier must participate co-operatively in any such review.\nA.C.6.3\tIf the Customer finds that the Supplier has failed to comply with the Child Protection Policy, the Supplier must promptly, and at the cost of the Supplier, take such actions as are required to ensure compliance with the Child Protection Policy.\nA.C.6.4\tIf an individual, the Supplier must sign and return to the Customer the Child Protection Professional Behaviours at Attachment B to the Child Protection Policy.\nA.C.6.5\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nA.C.7\tPreventing Sexual Exploitation Abuse and Harassment \nA.C.7.1\tThe Supplier must comply, and must ensure that its  officers, employees, agents and subcontractors comply with the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade?s Preventing Sexual Exploitation Abuse and Harassment Policy, accessible at https://dfat.gov.au/international-relations/themes/preventing-sexual-exploitation-abuse-and-harassment/Pages/default.aspx \nA.C.7.2\tThe Customer may conduct a review of the Supplier?s compliance with the Preventing Sexual Exploitation, Abuse and Harassment Policy. The Customer will give reasonable notice to the Supplier and the Supplier must participate co-operatively in any such review.\nA.C.7.3\tIf the Customer finds that the Supplier has failed to comply with the Preventing Sexual Exploitation, Abuse and Harassment Policy, the Supplier must promptly, and at the cost of the Supplier, take such actions as are required to ensure compliance with the Preventing Sexual Exploitation Abuse and Harassment Policy.\nA.C.7.4\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR POTENTIAL SUPPLIER\nBefore you start\nThis Response is not an offer for work.\nRead the all documentation provided by the Customer and distributed with this Response form and decide whether your organisation has the necessary skills and experience to meet the Customer?s requirement.\nDo not proceed further if:\nyour organisation cannot agree to the Commonwealth Contract Terms, available at https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-contracting-suite-ccs. These terms are non-negotiable and will form part of the Contract if you are successful in this ATM process.\nyour organisation does not meet and/or agree to the Minimum Content and Format Requirements and the Conditions for Participation (if any), set out at item A.A.1 [Key Information and Dates], as failure to do so will mean your response cannot be considered.\nyour organisation is a trust where the Trustee is not empowered to sign contracts on behalf of the Trust. Before proceeding, contact the Customer?s ATM Contact Officer at item\xa0A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer].\nyou are an Individual without an ABN and you do not meet the Australian Taxation Office?s (ATO) definition of an independent contractor available at: https://www.ato.gov.au/business/employee-or-contractor/how-to-work-it-out--employee-or-contractor. Before proceeding, contact the ATM Contact Officer set out at item\xa0A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer] to seek advice.\nThe Customer will evaluate all valid Responses received by the ATM Closing Time [Item A.A.1] that meet the Minimum Content and Format Requirements and the Conditions for Participation (if any), to determine which Potential Supplier has proposed the best value for money outcome for the Customer. Responses will be evaluated as per the criteria set out at Clause A.B.5 [Evaluation].\nParticipation in this ATM is at your organisation?s risk and cost. Please note this is a competitive process and your organisation may incur costs in responding. If you are unsuccessful, you will be unable to recoup these costs.\nFormat Requirements\nYou MUST use this form (CCS ATM Response Form) to submit your Response, which MUST comply with the Commonwealth ATM Terms, available at: https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-contracting-suite-ccs.\nAs this form is set out to facilitate the evaluation of responses, Potential Suppliers are to use the form as provided and are not permitted to make any changes to the structure or formatting of the document.\nA Potential Supplier?s Response to Parts 3, 4 and 5 of this form must not exceed six (6) A4 pages.\nA Potential Supplier?s Response to Part 3 must not exceed two (2) A4 pages.\nA Potential Supplier?s Response to Part 4 must not exceed three (3) A4 pages. \nA Potential Supplier?s Response to Part 5 must not exceed one (1) A4 page.\nGuidance for completing your Response\nSpecific questions about this ATM must be directed to the ATM Contact Officer set out at Item\xa0A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer] before Question Closing Date and Time set out at item A.A.1 [Key Information and Dates]. \nIn preparation of this Response, please note the Commonwealth Indigenous Procurement Policy (IPP) available at: https://www.niaa.gov.au/indigenous-affairs/economic-development/indigenous-procurement-policy-ipp may apply to the Customer in respect of this procurement. During evaluation of responses, the Customer may consider the Supplier?s ability to assist the Customer to meet its IPP obligations.\nEnsure your Response is as concise as possible while including all information that your organisation wants the evaluation team to consider. Do not include general marketing material or assume that the evaluation team has any knowledge of your organisation?s capabilities or personnel.\nDo not include pricing in any other part of your response except in the relevant Pricing Schedule.\nThe successful Supplier will have demonstrated its ability to provide the best value for the Customer. This will not necessarily be the lowest price.\nSubmit the form as required by Item A.A.4 [Lodgement of Responses].\nIf your organisation is unsuccessful with this Response, you may request a debrief to assist with future responses. The ATM Contact Officer set out at Item A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer] can arrange this for you.\nPotential Supplier?s Contact Officer\nFor all matters relating to this Response, the Potential Supplier?s Contact Officer is:\nPart 1 ? Potential Supplier?s Details\n1.1\tPotential Supplier?s Details\n1.2\tPrevious Judicial Decisions\n1.3\tWorkplace Gender Equality\n1.4\tIndigenous Businesses\n1.5\tAustralian Businesses\n1.6\tNew Zealand Businesses\n1.7\tPotential Supplier?s Contract Manager\nFor matters relating to the proposed Contract, the Potential Supplier?s Contract Manager will be:\n1.8\tPotential Supplier?s Address for Notices (if different from the Contract Manager)\nPart 2 ? Executive Summary\nNot Required. \nPart 3 ? Ability to Meet the Requirement (no more than 2 A4 pages)\n3.1\tDetailed Proposal to Meet the Customer?s Requirement \nCommonwealth Supplier Code of Conduct\nPart 4 ? Potential Supplier?s Demonstrated Capability and Capacity (no more than 3 A4 pages)\n4.1\tStatement of Skills and Experience\n4.2\tSpecified Personnel\n# if requested at A.A.2(b)\n4.3\tSubcontractors\nScope of Works to be Subcontracted\n4.4\tConflicts of Interest\nThe Potential Supplier has identified the following potential Conflicts of Interest and management strategies:\n4.5\tReferees\n4.6\tPre-existing Intellectual Property of Potential Supplier\n4.7\tConfidentiality of Potential Supplier?s Information\nThe Potential Supplier has assessed that the commercial information in the table below meets the requirements available at https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/buying-australian-government/additional-reporting-confidentiality and is seeking that information be kept confidential.\nPart 5 ? Total Costs to be incurred by the Customer (no more than 1 A4 page)\n5.1\tPricing \n5.2\tProposed Payment Schedule\n5.3\tAdditional Facilities and Assistance\n5.4\tNon-Compliance\n'}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1110.txt', 'extracted_text': "================================================================================\nAUSTENDER DETAILS: Current ATM View - DFAT-1110\n================================================================================\n\nDetail URL: https://www.tenders.gov.au/Atm/Show/a64467be-bb82-4228-85e7-5009fe1f162c\nScraped At: 2026-09-27 21:00:23\n\nSTRUCTURED ATTRIBUTES:\n----------------------------------------\nATM ID: DFAT-1110\nAgency: Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade - Australian Aid Program\nCategory: 80101600 - Project management\nClose Date & Time: 28-Sep-2026 10:00 am (ACT Local Time) Show close time for other time zones\nPublish Date: 2-Sep-2026\nLocation: ACT, NSW, VIC, SA, WA, QLD, NT, TAS Canberra, Sydney, Melbourne, Adelaide, Perth, Brisbane, Darwin, Hobart\nATM Type: Request for Tender\nMulti Agency Access: No\nPanel Arrangement: No\nMulti-stage: No\nDescription: The Mid-Term Review (MTR) of the Western Province Partnership (WPP) will assess the Program's progress toward its intended outcomes, identify key achievements and challenges, and provide actionable recommendations to inform management decisions and program improvement and consolidation opportunities for the remainder of WPP Phase II. The MTR will assess the relevance, effectiveness, efficiency, sustainability and cross-cutting performance (including GEDSI, subnational governance and climate resilience) of WPP?s approach and delivery.\nOther Instructions: \nConditions for Participation: Please refer to the attached ATM documents for full Conditions of Participation, and Minimum Content and Format Requirements.\nTimeframe for Delivery: 6 months (October 2026 to April 2027) with an option to extend for 3 months (April 2027 to July 2027). Proposed dates have been included in the ATM documentation.\nEstimated Value (AUD): From to $220,000.00\nAddress for Lodgement: www.tenders.gov.au\nAddenda Available: View Addenda\n\n================================================================================\n"}], 'content_hash': None, 'tags': None, 'first_seen_at': None, 'last_scanned_at': '2026-09-27T21:01:24.578472+00:00', 'updated_at': None, 'raw_extra': '{"tags": ["consulting", "professional services"]}', 'embedding': [0.05529860407114029, 0.016110684722661972, -0.030290622264146805, -0.006736882496625185, 0.03506741300225258, 0.061058301478624344, 0.031030509620904922, 0.03274824097752571, 0.05251004919409752, 0.04327768832445145, -0.01794968917965889, 0.06041096895933151, 0.017562691122293472, 0.05453575775027275, -0.010301951318979263, -0.01082936953753233, -0.01153761986643076, 0.04226714372634888, -0.06192648783326149, -0.014538425020873547, -0.016718221828341484, -0.09304096549749374, -0.018199525773525238, -0.0401308499276638, 0.03457912802696228, 0.00023201554722618312, -0.02334190346300602, 0.012567320838570595, -0.012369811534881592, -0.026145268231630325, 0.026407968252897263, -0.010507217608392239, 0.004628858063369989, -0.00028946142992936075, -0.0023380033671855927, 0.02503734640777111, 0.015290317125618458, 0.0069350325502455235, 0.043284911662340164, -0.